[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Angular Interceptors: Request and Response Interceptors"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors in Angular provide a powerful mechanism to intercept and modify HTTP requests and responses globally. They allow you to perform tasks such as adding headers, logging requests, handling errors, and caching responses before they reach your application components or after they return from the server. This tutorial will guide you through implementing request and response interceptors in Angular."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Interceptors"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors are classes that implement the `HttpInterceptor` interface. They sit between your application's HTTP requests and the backend server. Each outgoing request and incoming response passes through the interceptors, allowing you to modify them as needed."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Custom Interceptor"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a custom interceptor, you need to implement the `HttpInterceptor` interface and its `intercept` method. This method takes an `HttpRequest` object and an `HttpHandler` object as arguments. The `HttpRequest` represents the outgoing request, and the `HttpHandler` represents the next interceptor in the chain (or the HTTP backend if it's the last interceptor)."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpInterceptor, HttpRequest, HttpHandler, HttpEvent } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable()
export class AuthInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  intercept(req: HttpRequest<any>, next: HttpHandler): Observable<HttpEvent<any>> {
    // Modify the request (e.g., add an authorization header)
    const modifiedReq = req.clone({
      headers: req.headers.set('Authorization', 'Bearer your_token')
    });

    // Pass the modified request to the next handler
    return next.handle(modifiedReq);
  }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `AuthInterceptor` adds an `Authorization` header to every outgoing request. The `req.clone()` method is used to create a modified copy of the request, ensuring that the original request object is not mutated.  It's crucial to use `req.clone()` because `HttpRequest` objects are immutable."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Registering the Interceptor"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To make the interceptor effective, you need to register it as a provider in your Angular module.  This is done using the `HTTP_INTERCEPTORS` token."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { HttpClientModule, HTTP_INTERCEPTORS } from '@angular/common/http';
import { AuthInterceptor } from './auth.interceptor';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    // Your components
  ],
  imports: [
    BrowserModule,
    HttpClientModule
  ],
  providers: [
    {
      provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS,
      useClass: AuthInterceptor,
      multi: true
    }
  ],
  bootstrap: [/* Your bootstrap component */]
})
export class AppModule { }"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `multi: true` option allows you to register multiple interceptors. Angular will execute them in the order they are provided."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Response Interception"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors can also modify the response. To do this, you need to pipe the `Observable<HttpEvent<any>>` returned by `next.handle()` and use RxJS operators like `map`, `catchError`, or `tap`."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpInterceptor, HttpRequest, HttpHandler, HttpEvent, HttpResponse } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { tap } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Injectable()
export class LoggingInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  intercept(req: HttpRequest<any>, next: HttpHandler): Observable<HttpEvent<any>> {
    const startTime = Date.now();
    return next.handle(req)
      .pipe(
        tap(
          event => {
            if (event instanceof HttpResponse) {
              const endTime = Date.now();
              const elapsedTime = endTime - startTime;
              console.log(`Request for ${req.url} took ${elapsedTime} ms.`);
            }
          },
          error => {
            // Log the error
            console.error(`Request for ${req.url} failed: ${error.message}`);
          }
        )
      );
  }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `LoggingInterceptor` logs the time taken for each HTTP request to complete. The `tap` operator allows you to perform side effects (like logging) without modifying the response data.  The error handler within `tap` catches any errors that occur during the request."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Handling with Interceptors"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors are a great place to handle HTTP errors globally. You can use the `catchError` operator to catch errors and perform actions like displaying error messages or retrying the request."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpInterceptor, HttpRequest, HttpHandler, HttpEvent } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable, throwError } from 'rxjs';
import { catchError } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Injectable()
export class ErrorInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  intercept(req: HttpRequest<any>, next: HttpHandler): Observable<HttpEvent<any>> {
    return next.handle(req)
      .pipe(
        catchError((error) => {
          console.error('Error occurred', error);
          // Optionally, re-throw the error to propagate it to the component
          return throwError(() => error);
        })
      );
  }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `ErrorInterceptor` catches any errors that occur during the HTTP request and logs them to the console. It then re-throws the error using `throwError` so that the component that initiated the request can also handle the error.  The `throwError(() => error)` syntax is important to ensure proper type inference and avoid potential issues with error handling."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Important Considerations"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Interceptors are executed in the order they are provided in the `providers` array."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always clone the `HttpRequest` object using `req.clone()` before modifying it."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use RxJS operators like `map`, `tap`, and `catchError` to modify the response stream."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle errors gracefully and consider re-throwing them to allow components to handle them as well."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors are a powerful tool in Angular for globally modifying HTTP requests and responses. They can be used for a variety of tasks, including authentication, logging, error handling, and caching. By understanding how to create and register interceptors, you can significantly improve the maintainability and robustness of your Angular applications."}]
